\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Comparison of no drift versus drift scenarios. Left: When distributions overlap, the adversarial classifier cannot distinguish data sources (AUC $\approx $ 0.5). Right: When drift occurs, distributions shift and the classifier can distinguish sources (AUC $>>$ 0.5).}}{5}{figure.caption.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Overview of Explainable Adversarial Drift Detection (EADD) methodology. Blue boxes indicate novel contributions: Permutation Test (Step 3) and SHAP Feature Attribution (Step 4). The framework extends D3's adversarial validation with statistical rigor and root cause analysis.}}{18}{figure.caption.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Detection Delay Comparison by Temporal Drift Pattern. Lower values indicate faster detection. ``Failed'' cases are shown as 400 samples (maximum). EADD consistently achieves the lowest detection delay across all patterns.}}{32}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces SHAP Feature Importance Summary for Explainability Case Study. Feature F3* (Income) dominates with 55\% contribution, correctly identifying it as the sole drifting feature. This diagnostic output is unique to EADD---D3 and other methods provide no feature-level attribution.}}{34}{figure.caption.7}%
